course: books
lesson: 10
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter V, part 2: The mushroom, the long neck and the Pigeon
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The mushroom is perfectly round, so Alice cannot tell which side is which; she breaks off a bit from each side and nibbles the right-hand bit, and shrinks so fast that her chin hits her foot.
explain: Quickly she nibbles the left-hand bit, and her neck shoots up and up like a stalk, far above the treetops, bending about like a snake.
explain: A Pigeon flies at her face screaming "Serpent!": she has spent weeks guarding her eggs from serpents, and now one seems to have come wriggling down from the sky.
explain: Alice insists she is a little girl, but when she admits she has eaten eggs, the Pigeon decides that little girls must be a kind of serpent too.
explain: Nibbling first one bit and then the other, Alice gets back to her normal height, then makes herself nine inches tall so as not to scare whoever lives in a tiny house nearby: half of her plan is done.
fact: Alice looks at the mushroom and, "as it was perfectly round", finds it hard to work out which are its two sides.
fact: She breaks off a bit of the edge with each hand and asks "And now which is which?"
fact: Nibbling the right-hand bit, she feels "a violent blow underneath her chin: it had struck her foot!", because she is shrinking so fast.
fact: Squashed chin-to-foot, she just manages to swallow a morsel (a small bite) of the left-hand bit.
fact: Her head is free but her shoulders are "nowhere to be found"; she sees "an immense length of neck" rising "like a stalk out of a sea of green leaves", which are really the tops of the trees.
fact: Her neck bends "in any direction, like a serpent", and she curves it into "a graceful zigzag"; "serpent" is an old word for snake.
fact: A Pigeon beats her with its wings, screaming "Serpent!"; it has tried "the roots of trees", "banks" and "hedges" to keep its eggs safe.
fact: The Pigeon "haven't had a wink of sleep these three weeks", watching for serpents night and day while hatching its eggs.
fact: It had taken "the highest tree in the wood", and now a serpent seemed to come "wriggling down from the sky".
fact: Alice says "I—I'm a little girl", "rather doubtfully", because she has changed so many times that day.
fact: The Pigeon says "A likely story indeed!", which means: I don't believe you; it has seen many little girls, "but never one with such a neck as that!"
fact: Alice, "a very truthful child", admits she has tasted eggs, and the Pigeon says "if they do, why then they're a kind of serpent".
fact: The Pigeon adds "You're looking for eggs, I know that well enough; and what does it matter to me whether you're a little girl or a serpent?"
fact: Alice tells the Pigeon she is not looking for eggs, and would not want its eggs anyway: "I don't like them raw."; the Pigeon says sulkily "Well, be off, then!", and Alice crouches among the trees, her neck getting tangled in the branches.
fact: Nibbling one bit and then the other, she gets "down to her usual height", and says "there's half my plan done now!"
fact: She finds a little house "about four feet high", and, so as not to "frighten them out of their wits", she nibbles the right-hand bit until she is nine inches high.
fact: Old words in today's English: "a morsel" = a small bite, "indignantly" = crossly, because something seems unfair, "subdued" = quieter, "must needs" = just had to.
exercise: Why was it hard for Alice to find the two sides of the mushroom? | It was perfectly round | It was far too tall to reach | It kept moving about in the grass | It was covered in leaves
exercise: What did Alice do to get a bit from each side? | Stretched her arms round it and broke off the edge with each hand | Asked the Caterpillar to cut two pieces for her with its feet | Pulled the whole mushroom up out of the ground by its stalk | Climbed up on top of it and bit two pieces out of the middle
exercise: Which bit did Alice nibble first? | The right-hand bit | The bit on the left side | The bit in the middle | The thick white stalk
exercise: What happened when she nibbled the first bit? | She shrank so fast her chin hit her foot | Her neck shot up above the trees at once | She fell fast asleep right under the mushroom | Nothing happened at all for several minutes
exercise: Why was it hard to eat the second bit? | Her chin was pressed so close to her foot she could hardly open her mouth | The bit had dropped into the grass and she could not find it there | The Caterpillar had taken the second bit away with it into the grass | The bit was hard and dry, and much too tough to chew properly
exercise: What happened after she ate the left-hand bit? | Her neck grew immensely long | She shrank to just one inch high | She turned into a real serpent | She fell back into the pool of tears
exercise: What did Alice see far below her? | A sea of green leaves | The pool of tears | The White Rabbit's house | A big blue sky
exercise: What were the green leaves really? | The tops of the trees | A big field of cabbages | The Caterpillar's mushroom | Leaves of a giant thistle
exercise: What could Alice's neck do now? | Bend about in any direction, like a serpent | Stretch out sideways, like a garden hose | Fold up neatly, like a little telescope | Turn right round, just like an owl's head
exercise: Who flew into Alice's face? | A large pigeon | A little bat | The Eaglet | An old magpie from the bank
exercise: What did the Pigeon scream at Alice? | "Serpent!" | "Egg thief!" | "You giant!" | "Mary Ann!"
exercise: What does "serpent" mean? | A snake | A servant | A dragon | A big giant
exercise: Which places had the Pigeon tried to keep its eggs safe? | Roots of trees, banks and hedges | Chimneys, roofs and garden walls | Ponds, rivers and the pool of tears | The Rabbit's house and the hall
exercise: How long had the Pigeon gone without sleep? | Three weeks | Three whole days | Three months | Three long hours
exercise: Where had the Pigeon made its nest at last? | In the highest tree in the wood | In a hole under a mushroom | On the roof of the Rabbit's house | In the long grass by the thistle
exercise: Why did the Pigeon think Alice came "down from the sky"? | Her head was high above the treetops | She had fallen out of an aeroplane | She was riding on the Eaglet's back | She was flying a large paper kite high over the wood
exercise: How did Alice say "I'm a little girl"? | Rather doubtfully | Very proudly | In a whisper | In French, to be polite
exercise: Here "A likely story indeed!" means... | I don't believe you | What a lovely story | Tell me another story | That is very likely true
exercise: Why didn't the Pigeon believe Alice was a little girl? | It had never seen a girl with such a neck | It had never seen a little girl before | Alice had feathers in her hair | Alice was hissing like a snake
exercise: What did Alice truthfully admit? | She had tasted eggs | She had stolen some eggs | She liked eggs best raw | She had a pet snake at home
exercise: What did the Pigeon decide about little girls? | If they eat eggs, they are a kind of serpent | They are all very kind and helpful | They never eat eggs of any kind at all | They are a kind of pigeon without wings
exercise: Why did Alice say she would not want the Pigeon's eggs? | She did not like them raw | She was not hungry | She had her own eggs | She only ever ate magic cake
exercise: What kept happening to Alice's neck as she crouched among the trees? | It got tangled in the branches | It kept growing longer and longer | It kept falling fast asleep | It turned green like the leaves
exercise: How did Alice get back to her usual height? | Nibbling first one bit and then the other | Drinking from a bottle with no label | Eating one of the pebble-cakes | Waiting quietly for the magic to wear off by itself
exercise: How high was the little house Alice found? | About four feet | About nine feet | About nine inches | About two feet
exercise: How tall did Alice make herself before going near the house? | Nine inches | Four feet | Three inches | Ten feet
exercise: Thinking back: what was the Caterpillar's last clue about the mushroom? | One side makes you taller, the other shorter | Eat it all at once or nothing will happen | Only the stalk will change your size | It will make you exactly three inches tall, just like me
exercise: Thinking back to chapter IV: what were the two parts of Alice's plan? | Get to her right size, then find the lovely garden | Find the Rabbit, then return his fan and gloves | Make friends with the Mouse, then hear its tale | Go home to Dinah, then tell her sister all about it
test: Josh goes: "Alice ate the left bit first and went tiny." Put him right. | The right bit shrank her first; the left bit made her neck long | She ate both bits together and stayed the same size | The left bit shrank her and the right bit made her neck long | She never ate the mushroom, the Caterpillar ate it
test: Destiny, Gen Z: "The Pigeon was so pressed, screaming 'Serpent!' at a whole girl." ("pressed" = upset, stressed.) Why was the Pigeon so stressed? | It had guarded its eggs from serpents for weeks without sleep | Alice had knocked its nest out of the tree with her head | The Caterpillar had told it a serpent was coming that day | It was late for the Duchess's party and had lost its gloves
test: Why is the Pigeon's thinking wrong? | Two things that both eat eggs are not the same thing | Little girls never, ever eat eggs at all | Serpents do not eat eggs, only leaves | Pigeons cannot think about anything
test: What would have happened if Alice had eaten only the left-hand bit after that? | Her neck would have grown even longer | She would have shrunk back to nothing | She would have turned into a pigeon | She would have stayed just the same
test: Auntie Sue from Manchester: "She's dead honest, our Alice, admits she's had eggs an' all." ("dead" = very, "an' all" = as well.) Why does Alice admit it? | She is a very truthful child | She wants the Pigeon's eggs | She is trying to trick it | She is hungry for eggs
test: True or false: Alice is completely sure she is a little girl. | False: she says it "rather doubtfully", after so many changes | True: she says it loudly and proudly at once | True: she shows the Pigeon her school book | False: she says she is really a serpent
test: Liam, rough: "Get lost, you sneaky snake!" Which of the Pigeon's lines is closest to this? | "Well, be off, then!" | "A likely story indeed!" | "Serpent, I say again!" | "I don't believe it"
test: Why does Alice make herself nine inches before visiting the little house? | So she won't frighten whoever lives there | So she can fit in through the chimney | So the Pigeon cannot see her any more | So she can break off a bit of the house and eat it like a cake
test: Why does the Pigeon say it does not matter whether Alice is a girl or a serpent? | Either way, it thinks she is after its eggs | It likes girls and serpents just the same | It is too sleepy to care about anything | Its eggs have already hatched and flown
test: Pete from Boston: "That neck was wicked long, kid." ("wicked" = very, in New England.) What did the long neck look like from above? | A stalk rising out of a sea of green leaves | A ladder leaning against the Rabbit's house | A ribbon tied in a bow round a tree | A rope hanging down the deep well
test: Looking back to chapter V part 1: the Caterpillar asked "Who are you?". How does that question come back here? | Alice is unsure what she is when the Pigeon asks | The Pigeon asks the Caterpillar who Alice is | Alice asks the Pigeon who it is, very politely | The White Rabbit comes by and asks Alice the very same question again
test: Looking back to chapter I: Alice wished she could "shut up like a telescope". What does she do here? | Opens out like a telescope, in her neck | Shuts up neatly like a telescope | Looks through a telescope at the garden | Finds a telescope on top of the mushroom
test: Looking back over the whole book: which of these has NOT changed Alice's size so far? | The comfits | The DRINK ME bottle | The fan | The pebble-cakes
test: How is Alice different now from chapter I, where she cried because she could not control her size? | She can now choose her size with the two bits of mushroom | She has stopped caring about her size altogether | She has given up and decided to stay nine feet tall | She still has no way at all of changing her size
test: ★★★ The Pigeon says: serpents eat eggs; little girls eat eggs; so little girls are serpents. Which argument has the SAME mistake? | Cats have tails; dogs have tails; so dogs are cats | All birds have feathers; a duck is a bird; so it has feathers | Fish live in water; a shark is a fish; so it lives in water | Rabbits are animals; animals breathe; so rabbits breathe
test: ★★★ To shrink from her usual height down to nine inches near the house, which bit did Alice nibble? Use the first nibble as your clue. | The right-hand bit | The left-hand bit | Both bits at once | Neither bit; she waited
test: ★★★ Count the places the Pigeon names for keeping its eggs safe: roots of trees, banks, hedges, and finally the highest tree. How many is that? | Four | Three | Five | Two
exam: Identify the effect produced upon the protagonist by consumption of the right-hand portion of the mushroom. | A rapid diminution of stature | An elongation of the neck | An immediate loss of speech | A transformation into a serpent
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the Pigeon identified the protagonist as a serpent. | The excessive length of her neck and her presence among the treetops | Her repeated hissing at the Pigeon from beneath the nest | Her statement that she had a fondness for raw eggs | The scales visible upon her arms and her forked tongue
exam: It is given that the protagonist admitted to having tasted eggs. Determine the conclusion drawn therefrom by the Pigeon. | That little girls constitute a kind of serpent | That the protagonist was undoubtedly a pigeon | That eggs are unsuitable for consumption | That serpents are a kind of little girl
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the protagonist's stature upon approaching the small house. | Nine inches | Four feet | Three inches | Two feet
